this file storage necessary?

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:14.680
I mean, when I
think of, you know,

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.780
working directly in the cloud,
you can have virtual machines.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:19.670
You can add on disks
to those for storage.

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:22.220
And then anything beyond that
need, we have Google Cloud

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:22.880
Storage, right?

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.255
So you've been
able to, you know,

00:09:24.255 --> 00:09:26.090
create buckets and
dump files in there.

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:28.100
That seems to be
pretty comprehensive.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.400
What is it that those
two aren't covering?

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.400
DEAN HILDEBRAND:
Yeah, so I joined--

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.430
actually exactly two
years ago this week

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.048
is my Google anniversary and--

00:09:36.048 --> 00:09:37.340
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh, congrats.

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:37.630
[LAUGHS]

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:38.260
DEAN HILDEBRAND: Oh, thanks.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:39.980
Yeah, it's a nice coincidence.

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.440
And ever since I joined,
the one number 1 thing we

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.030
heard from enterprises
was, you know,

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:48.980
we want to be able to port our
applications into Google Cloud.

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:50.992
What is the mechanism
you're giving us to do so?

00:09:50.992 --> 00:09:52.950
And we heard this over
and over and over again.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:56.000
And so that was where, you know,
the real genesis of the NetApp

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.590
partnership started.

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:00.170
But based on that, you know,
what the customers also

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:01.960
said was, don't
give me, you know,

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.610
a solution, where it looks
like two products that

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:07.190
have been mushed
together that you

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:09.065
have a great platform
with all the services.

00:10:09.065 --> 00:10:10.940
And then, there's this
thing off to the side.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:15.070
What they wanted to see was
native services, or at least

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.990
look native, and they were
supported in a native way.

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:20.030
And they provided all
of the same, you know,

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:23.540
great features and availability
and everything else that

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:25.823
Google provides in its
native services in any

00:10:25.823 --> 00:10:27.490
of the partnerships
that we're building.

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:30.660
And so this is where we
really got the two together.

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:32.493
And so why file stores, though?

00:10:32.493 --> 00:10:33.660
Why were they asking for it?

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:36.680
And that's because that's the
dominant storage on-premise.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:39.650
And so as part of the cloud,
when we're talking enterprises,

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:41.850
they're like, well,
this is what we have.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:43.610
We don't have an object store.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.380
We have file storage on-prem.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:46.680
We have block storage.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.790
This is where our databases
are running, our ERP systems.

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:52.948
All of the critical systems that
are running the enterprises,

00:10:52.948 --> 00:10:54.240
that's what they're running on.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:58.460
And so over time, they might
consider about a modernization

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:01.610
and thinking about how they can
build in Google Cloud Storage

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:02.970
in one way or the other.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:06.830
But in what they want to do in
terms of leveraging the cloud

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:09.355
and really making
the most use of it--

00:11:09.355 --> 00:11:10.730
they just want to
have, you know,

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:14.160
the same high-quality storage
that NetApp is offering them

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:14.660
on-prem.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:16.920
And they want that in Google.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.940
And so in the meantime, these
applications-- they've all

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:22.190
been built for that, you
know, trillion dollar

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:23.990
on-premise enterprise market.

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:26.180
And that entire
trillion dollar industry

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:29.490
is not going to be rewritten in
the next, you know, few years.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:32.300
And so we really need
to build partnerships

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:34.580
with leaders in the
space, like NetApp

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:38.120
that can provide all of
the high-quality durability

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:40.930
and availability of the storage
that they expect on-prem

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:41.880
and now in the cloud.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.283
ALIM KARIM: And just to add
a few more comments to that,

00:11:44.283 --> 00:11:46.700
right, so really-- and when
you think about the enterprise

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:50.900
space, to Dean's point, this is
about an application ecosystem

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:53.893
that supports business
processes within an enterprise.

00:11:53.893 --> 00:11:56.060
And these are workloads
that have typically not been

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:57.640
candidates for cloud, right?

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.080
And now we are essentially
enabling customers

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:02.000
to make that
transition so that they

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.340
don't have to do data
plane re-architecture.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:05.340
That's point number 1.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:06.830
Point number 2
is, hey, if you've

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:10.820
got a new cloud user, as you
said, you could spin up a VM.

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:12.530
You can attach some disks to it.

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:13.650
What do you typically do?

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:16.130
You put a file system
on those disks, right?

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:18.110
And then you read
and write data to it.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:20.035
And now, you've
got a VM and a PD

00:12:20.035 --> 00:12:21.410
that you've got
to manage, right?

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:24.230
Our point is if you're going
to end up just putting a file

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:27.080
system on this, why not just
give you a file system that you

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:29.090
can attach to your
VM fully managed?

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:30.890
And you can take
it to any other VM

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:33.675
without having to worry about
taking snapshots of that PD

00:12:33.675 --> 00:12:35.300
and doing a whole
bunch of other things

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:37.460
that you would do to
maintain that file system.

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:39.410
Just get the service
into the cloud.

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:40.320
Get the service.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:42.737
DEAN HILDEBRAND: And there are
some practical things, too.

00:12:42.737 --> 00:12:45.050
It's interesting when
people think about, well, we

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:47.900
have Google Cloud Storage and
all the amazing applications

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:48.650
that are using it.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:50.600
But then, they forget
that when you boot a VM,

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:52.800
you see a file system.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:54.770
And you log into that VM.

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:57.230
And you're still
using a file system.

00:12:57.230 --> 00:13:00.110
And then the applications that
you run, such as, you know,

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.060
building your
applications and the whole

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:05.620
CI/CD pipeline that you
might implement across

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:07.790
for your organization--

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:09.660
all using file system.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.300
And so I think we
forget a lot of times

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:15.110
that how pervasive file
systems are in our daily lives

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:18.290
and how we really need to make
them a first-class citizen

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:19.685
inside of Google Cloud.

00:13:19.685 --> 00:13:20.300
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Yeah, I mean, I

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:22.850
think it goes to show you that
all the layers of abstraction

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:26.120
have really made you completely
re-evaluate where you actually

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:28.190
think about what you're
trying to do, right?

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.680
And when you have all of
these virtual machines, which

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.260
are themselves already
heavily abstracted with all

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:36.002
these managed services, you're
so far away from the file

00:13:36.002 --> 00:13:37.460
system that I think,
like you said,

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:38.918
you just kind of
forget about them.

00:13:38.918 --> 00:13:41.200
So you mentioned lift and
shift a couple of times.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.170
That is a major
component, right,

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:45.500
because these enterprise
companies or these larger

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:48.530
architectures that have
been built based on existing

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:50.050
file system
solutions-- you know,

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:52.488
they can move to the cloud
using some of these solutions.

00:13:52.488 --> 00:13:54.530
What about people who
aren't lifted and shifting,

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.840
but actually building new
applications in the cloud?

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:57.743
Is it still relevant?

00:13:57.743 --> 00:13:58.910
DEAN HILDEBRAND: Definitely.

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:03.050
And part of that reason is that
the basic fundamental about how

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:05.900
we build and deliver
services isn't changing,

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:09.080
even when they are developing
new apps in the cloud.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.340
So we've talked to
banks, for example,

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:13.850
where they want to do new
products and new developments

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:15.513
strictly inside of Google Cloud.

00:14:15.513 --> 00:14:17.180
When they're doing
that process, they're

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:19.190
not lifting and shifting
existing applications.

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:21.622
But they're still going
through the same CI/CD pipeline

00:14:21.622 --> 00:14:23.330
that they would use
somewhere else, where

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:24.770
they need file storage.

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:27.860
They still need
databases and other ways

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:29.690
of managing their
data, which require

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:32.390
low latency and the
types of semantics

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:34.070
that file systems
offer them in terms

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:35.310
of managing those systems.

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:38.690
And so even though the
applications aren't necessarily

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:42.590
literally taken from the
on-prem into the cloud,

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:44.240
the new ones that they write--

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:46.490
there's fundamentally
no reason why

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:49.670
they would try to avoid file
storage when, especially

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:52.040
with NetApp, we can
make it so easy to use

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.450
that you kind of, again, sort
of forget it's even there.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:55.760
ALIM KARIM: One last thing
to add there [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:57.800
and I think there's also
a class of applications

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:00.590
that we're seeing, specifically
in the analytics space,

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:03.230
where you've got n
number of machines trying

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:06.230
to do machine learning or
something of that sort that

00:15:06.230 --> 00:15:09.800
use libraries that actually
interact with a file share.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:12.560
So another data point that
I usually like to point out

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:15.530
is that Google has done a great
job of making GitHub metadata

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:17.390
publicly available on BigQuery.

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:19.010
So if you just look
at that data set

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:21.560
and you look at sort of the
two big programming languages--

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:23.450
Java and Python as an example--

00:15:23.450 --> 00:15:26.030
and you look at all
of the new code that's

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:28.830
been written over the
last two, three years

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:30.470
and look at Java
and Python and see

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:33.380
how many file calls those
applications are making,

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:34.380
it's over half.

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:36.740
Like, over half of
the source code files

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:38.600
are actually making
file I/O calls.

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:40.700
And really, if you
look a little bit more,

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:43.940
it's mainly in this machine
learning analytics space,

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:45.500
where they're using
those libraries

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:48.140
and interacting with data
that's on a file system.

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:48.950
So there you go.

00:15:48.950 --> 00:15:50.240
File comes back.

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:51.698
DEAN HILDEBRAND:
And actually, I'll

00:15:51.698 --> 00:15:54.530
add to that that TensorFlow
has two primary ways

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:55.580
of accessing storage--

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:58.205
through object storage and,
again, back through on-file.

00:15:58.205 --> 00:16:01.040
And so even though there's
many applications that

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:03.050
are lift and shifted,
as well, one of those

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:05.360
is, in fact, TensorFlow
applications.

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:08.030
And so if people have
developed their machine

00:16:08.030 --> 00:16:12.010
learning pipeline on-premise,
in many ways when they move it

00:16:12.010 --> 00:16:15.010
down into the cloud, the
shift over into object storage

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:16.600
becomes a difficult move.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:19.090
And so then, they are,
again, in a situation,

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:22.870
where you want to build a hybrid
cloud with a common storage API

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:25.790
across both on-premise and in
the cloud-- you know, again,

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:26.720
back to file storage--

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:27.220
[LAUGHS]

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:27.430
--again.

00:16:27.430 --> 00:16:29.472
JON FOUST: You kind of
caught me right off guard.

00:16:29.472 --> 00:16:32.497
I was really about to ask
you a question about on-prem.

00:16:32.497 --> 00:16:33.455
You got me there, Dean.

00:16:33.455 --> 00:16:34.060
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:35.310
DEAN HILDEBRAND: Ask it again.

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:36.340
I'll reword it again.

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:38.723
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:38.723 --> 00:16:40.390
JON FOUST: Alim, you
mentioned something

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:41.473
that really caught my eye.

00:16:41.473 --> 00:16:43.330
And maybe this
will, you know, help

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:46.240
us transition a little segue
about NetApp in the cloud.

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.000
The file storage is
pretty much managed cloud.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:51.070
But what I'm curious
about is what

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:53.680
is different about that
and NetApp than on-premise?

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:56.740
And are there really
pros and cons to it?

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:59.180
And maybe dean can also chime
in and answer another one

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:00.430
of my questions ahead of time.

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:01.693
[LAUGHS]

00:17:01.693 --> 00:17:03.860
ALIM KARIM: [INAUDIBLE] no,
that's a great question.

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:06.339
So if you think about, you
know, the environments Dean

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:08.130
was mentioning
previously on-prem,

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:12.369
there is a long amount of time
and a large amount of effort

00:17:12.369 --> 00:17:15.730
to actually stand up a service
that, you know, an application

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:19.119
owner on-premise could use,
for example, file services

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:19.900
specifically.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:22.599
What we're doing with Cloud
and why this is so different

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:25.390
is that that same
application owner that

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:27.730
needs file storage
for his app can

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:30.385
get that in the cloud
in GCP natively,

00:17:30.385 --> 00:17:33.190
you know, in a matter of
seconds, minutes, let's

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:36.220
say, for if you really
click through the stuff.

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:39.400
The time to consume is
really, really short, right?

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:41.060
So that's one key benefit.

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:42.490
The second is this
is now hitting

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:45.370
on the aspect of it being a
fully managed service, right?

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:49.180
So if you contrast this with the
traditional NetApp customers--

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:52.660
they would essentially acquire
software and systems that

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:55.300
ran storage and then put
them in a data center,

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:57.640
then connect them to a
network, then install

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:00.470
application servers that
talk to that infrastructure,

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:02.110
and then over a
number of years have

00:18:02.110 --> 00:18:04.480
to worry about
expansion of capacity,

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:08.200
patching that infrastructure,
scaling that infrastructure,

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:11.770
refreshing that infrastructure
when its Capex life ran out.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:15.430
All of those things essentially
go away from a managed service

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:16.660
perspective, right?

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:20.430
So in the managed service
context, a application owner,

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:23.080
a developer-- whoever is
using this in the cloud--

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:25.240
just gets that final
endpoint without having

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.520
to worry about any of
those infrastructure

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:29.020
burdens behind the scenes.

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:31.210
And not only that
is the entry point

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:33.730
to using these becomes
much more tangible--

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:37.090
instead of buying, you know,
maybe a petabyte or two

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:39.820
of capacity, you can
just essentially use

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:42.970
the service to provision
a terabyte or, you know,

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:45.850
even smaller units of capacity
and just get going and just

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:47.500
pay for that little
piece that you

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:49.330
need without having
to worry about

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:51.830
six months of installation
and lead time, right?

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:54.490
So really minimizing
the time it takes

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:58.120
to get actual access to your
storage and then second,

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:00.610
abstracting away all
of the infrastructure

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:04.300
or low-value pieces that go
into running the infrastructure

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:06.160
is what the fully
managed service provides.

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:06.760
DEAN HILDEBRAND:
I would just add

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:09.400
to that, on-premise you
would ask the administrator,

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:13.240
OK, what is the SLO
of your NetApp box

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:14.740
or of your storage system?

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:17.770
These are not typical questions
that either are asked,

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:20.630
nor do they have the
ability to answer.

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:22.840
There is a lot of
monitoring that

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:25.510
is in place that in the
cloud that just isn't there

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:27.730
on-prem in terms of
how they understand

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:31.690
and how they think about
delivering storage services.

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:33.190
And I think that's
where really when

