00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:10.690
How do I authenticate my Google
Kubernetes Engine cluster

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:11.710
in a CI/CD pipeline?

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:12.710
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:17.030
So I mean, this is a very common
part of hooking up Kubernetes

00:32:17.030 --> 00:32:19.098
to CI/CD, because usually
when you're doing this,

00:32:19.098 --> 00:32:21.140
you're using, like, a
headless environment, where

00:32:21.140 --> 00:32:23.630
you don't necessarily have
the same set of tools.

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:26.180
Or you're running on a very
small box or any number

00:32:26.180 --> 00:32:28.880
of things that you don't have
your full environment set up

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:29.540
for.

00:32:29.540 --> 00:32:31.940
And you could spin up
the G Cloud environment

00:32:31.940 --> 00:32:34.238
and authenticate that way,
so the G Cloud OFF command,

00:32:34.238 --> 00:32:35.780
which I think is
what a lot of people

00:32:35.780 --> 00:32:37.353
do from their
local environments.

00:32:37.353 --> 00:32:39.020
But when you're running
this, especially

00:32:39.020 --> 00:32:41.390
in a CI/CD case with your
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:44.100
needing to authenticate every
time you push up a new build,

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:45.850
you don't really want
to have to configure

00:32:45.850 --> 00:32:47.390
your environment
every time or have

00:32:47.390 --> 00:32:49.010
a lot of requirements for it.

00:32:49.010 --> 00:32:51.740
So there's this great blog
post from [? Ahmet ?] talking

00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:53.840
about the easiest
solution here, which

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:55.910
is to create a
kubeconfig file that

00:32:55.910 --> 00:32:57.557
includes the authentication.

00:32:57.557 --> 00:32:59.390
And this is something
that you can basically

00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:00.710
check into your repo.

00:33:00.710 --> 00:33:03.020
It's got the current
information for your master

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:04.430
in there-- or your cluster.

00:33:04.430 --> 00:33:07.580
And then you, as a secret,
provide the service account.

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:09.050
So you create a service account.

00:33:09.050 --> 00:33:10.372
You get the secret key file.

00:33:10.372 --> 00:33:12.830
You treat that like a secret,
because it's, well, a secret.

00:33:12.830 --> 00:33:13.530
[DING]

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:14.030
[LAUGHS]

00:33:14.030 --> 00:33:15.960
And then in your
kubeconfig file,

00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:17.540
you actually have
the authentication

00:33:17.540 --> 00:33:19.280
against your cluster
that will actually

00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:22.230
use the environment variables
to pull that authentication.

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:24.200
So this gives you a little
bit more flexibility

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:27.170
in, for example, your CI/CD
pipeline, because now,

00:33:27.170 --> 00:33:29.360
wherever you're actually
running that build, you

00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:31.430
don't need to install a gcloud.

00:33:31.430 --> 00:33:33.080
You don't need to go
through any steps.

00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:35.540
You just need to have this
very, very short snippet

00:33:35.540 --> 00:33:38.000
for the kubeconfig file
and then your-- obviously

00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:41.250
your secret config file for
the actual authentication.

00:33:41.250 --> 00:33:42.380
So check out the blog post.

00:33:42.380 --> 00:33:44.330
I think it's really
quick, really simple.

00:33:44.330 --> 00:33:46.910
And you know, for anyone
who is figuring out

00:33:46.910 --> 00:33:49.450
how to build their
pipeline with containers,

00:33:49.450 --> 00:33:52.170
this will probably make that
process just a little quicker.

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:52.880
JON FOUST: That was
